The AI usage metrics (src/qrh/usage/, docs/usage.md) follow a design by Yoichi Palacios Tanaka,
published as claude-kit (https://github.com/IchiSieben/claude-kit, Apache License 2.0):
harvesting Claude Code transcripts into per-session metrics, counting each message id once,
taking cost from the harness cost-state, the cache and context measures, and the idea of
deterministic "plays" that name their rule and numbers.

No source code from claude-kit is included; the implementation here was written independently
for this project's own data model, thresholds files and reports.

Human approval tokens (src/qrh/approvals.py, src/qrh/hooks/approval_guard.py, docs/hooks.md "Approvals") adapt the
design of agent-guardrail by Jose Carlos Gonzales Tanaka (MIT, https://github.com/jcgtanaka/agent-guardrail): out-of-band
pending requests, HMAC-signed single-use tokens, and the rule that the agent must not be able to approve itself.

The runaway guard (src/qrh/hooks/runaway.py), the claim check and weakened-test audit (src/qrh/hooks/claim_check.py,
src/qrh/checks/weakened_tests.py), the waste report (src/qrh/usage/waste.py) and the regression finder
(src/qrh/usage/regress.py) follow the ideas of the claim-check, runaway-guard, session-waste-report and regression-finder
skills by Ryan Alberts (MIT, https://github.com/RyanAlberts/best-of-Agent-Harnesses, skills folder only):
loop, failure-streak and spend trip wires; checking "tests pass" claims against the transcript and the weakened-test
signals (deleted tests, removed assertions, new skips); the waste categories (re-reads, oversized results, cache
rebuilds, failing loops, compactions); and before/after comparison per turn with Mann-Whitney U and Fisher exact tests.
The code, thresholds, data model and report text here were written independently; no source file was copied, and the
catalog text of that repository (CC BY-SA) was not used.

Third-party dependencies

The harness needs the runtime packages below. None is copied into this repository; they are installed
separately. Versions and licences were read from the installed package metadata of the project environment.
All are permissive and compatible with Apache-2.0. No GPL or other copyleft licence was found.

- pandas 3.1.0: BSD-3-Clause. Permissive, compatible.
- numpy 2.5.4: BSD-3-Clause AND 0BSD AND MIT AND Zlib AND CC0-1.0 (the last four cover bundled parts). All permissive, compatible.
- typer 0.27.3: MIT. Permissive, compatible.
- pydantic 2.14.1: MIT. Permissive, compatible.
- pyyaml 6.0.3: MIT. Permissive, compatible.
- pyarrow 26.0.0: Apache-2.0. Same licence as this project, compatible.
- matplotlib 3.11.2: Matplotlib licence (PSF-based, listed as "Python Software Foundation License"). Permissive, compatible.
- scipy 1.18.1: BSD-3-Clause (the package lists "BSD License"; its licence file also covers bundled parts, which were not reviewed one by one). Permissive, compatible.
- cryptography 50.0.2: Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause (dual licence, either may be chosen). Permissive, compatible.

Their transitive runtime dependencies (from uv.lock) were checked the same way: all are MIT, MIT-0, MIT-CMU, BSD,
ISC, PSF-2.0 or Apache-2.0 licensed. None is copyleft.

Development tools (pytest, hypothesis) are not part of the runtime and are not listed here.
